<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Swa Saw Ke</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1330">1330</a>–<a href="/years/1400">1400</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Swa Saw Ke was king of Ava in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-burma">Upper Burma</a> from 1367 to 1400, consolidating the kingdom founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thado-minbya">Thado Minbya</a> in 1365. He brought much of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> under Ava's control and in 1385 began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/forty-years-war">Forty Years' War</a> against the Mon kingdom of Hanthawaddy under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/razadarit">Razadarit</a>, which his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minkhaung-i">Minkhaung I</a> continued. His long reign laid the foundations of Ava as the leading power of Upper Burma.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>He was born Saw Ke on 16 July 1330 at Thayet, then a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of Pinya, to its ruling family: his father, Governor Min Shin Saw of Thayet, was a son of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyawswa-of-pagan">Kyawswa of Pagan</a>. In January 1334 the Arakanese raided Thayet and carried the governor's whole family to Launggyet, where the children were educated by a learned monk. Returning to Pinya in 1343/44, Saw Ke became governor of Talok, then of Yamethin. After quarrelling with King Kyawswa II of Pinya he defected to Sagaing, whose king made him governor of Amyint.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thado-minbya">Thado Minbya</a> died of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> in September 1367, the court first offered the throne to Thilawa of Yamethin, who refused and named Saw Ke. He became king on 5 September 1367, retook Sagaing from his sister Queen Saw Omma, and won over the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> by diplomacy. Crowned in March 1368 as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mingyi-swa">Mingyi Swa</a>, he relied on the advice of his court, led by Chief Minister Min Yaza, a commoner.</p><p>He dealt with his neighbours one at a time. A treaty of 1370/71 with King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/binnya-u">Binnya U</a> of Ramanya fixed the southern border. In the north he drove the Maw Shans out of Myedu by early 1373, and an uneasy ceasefire followed. He placed nominees on the throne of Arakan from 1373, and had Governor Pyanchi I of Toungoo ambushed and killed near Prome in 1375.</p><p>In 1385 he accepted an appeal from Laukpya, lord of Myaungmya, against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/razadarit">Razadarit</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bago">Pegu</a>. Invasions in 1385, 1386 and 1390, the second and third led by Swa himself on the river with 17,000 men, all failed, and he accepted a truce in which Pegu returned Gu-Htut. In 1392–93 Thilawa destroyed all 15 regiments of a Maw army at Shangon, ending the Maw raids for the rest of the reign.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Swa died in April 1400 after a reign of 32 years and was succeeded by Tarabya. He had not restored the Pagan Empire, but he left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-burma">Upper Burma</a> united and stable for the first time since the fall of Pagan in the 1280s. The peace allowed irrigation to be repaired and farmland abandoned since the Mongol invasions to be reclaimed, building up the economy and manpower that later kings of Ava used to expand.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Swa Saw Ke” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Swa Saw Ke</b> became king of Ava, the kingdom founded by Thado Minbya in 1365, and restored central authority in Upper Burma for the first time since the fall of Pagan.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-5">5 September in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Swa Saw Ke</b> and his son Minkhaung I fought the Forty Years' War against Razadarit of the Hanthawaddy Kingdom from 1385 to 1424.</blockquote><a href="/state/ava-kingdom">Ava Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Thayet, in what is now <a href="/countries/myanmar">Myanmar</a>. Died in Inwa, in what is now <a href="/countries/myanmar">Myanmar</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=19.32,95.18&amp;year=1375" aria-label="Thayet on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Swa Saw Ke"><image href="/maps/globes/g090n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5418 5899h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5418 5899h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=19.32,95.18&amp;year=1375">What was here in 1375? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
